Short:        SSH2 client/server suite, security fixes
Author:       Stefan "Bebbo" Franke (bebbossh), fixes by Matthias Muench
Uploader:     bebbossh@amigaworld.de (Matthias Muench)
Type:         comm/net
Version:      1.46.1
Requires:     68020+ (programs), AmigaOS 3.x, bsdsocket.library;
              optional: codesets.library 6+
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0
Distribution: Aminet

This is an UNOFFICIAL modified build of bebbossh by Stefan "Bebbo" Franke:
the 1.45 sources with the fixes below and the upstream 1.46 key-exchange
fix merged (0017), marked as version 1.46.1 (0019). Included are the changed
programs bebbossh, bebboscp, bebbosshkeygen and the server bebbosshd
(68020 builds) and libcryptossh.library in the same three variants as the
original release:

  libcryptossh.library      68000 code, runs everywhere
  libcryptossh.library020   for 68020-68040 and the 68080 (Vampire)
  libcryptossh.library060   for the 68060

Install ONE of them as LIBS:libcryptossh.library (see INSTALLATION).
Read the SECURITY note before running bebbosshd.

Also in the archive, like the upstream release: the native Linux builds of
the four programs (linux/), server templates (template/sshd_config,
template/passwd), the xterm-amiga terminfo (compiled and source), the
workbench icon, Bebbo's original 1.46 readme (bebbossh.readme.orig) and
all source changes as patches (src/).

Note: the programs are 68020 builds (the original ships 68000 programs).
During the first tests a 68000 build showed sporadic 4-byte memory
corruption on the 68080 (about 2 of 13 transfers, and only with the 68000
programs together with the generic 68000 library). This was then chased in
depth on the real V4SA and could NOT be reproduced: ~230 transfers stayed
clean (including the exact build that first failed, both ciphers, up- and
downloads), a standalone stress test of the generic library's crypto ran
10 million operations (AES, Poly1305, x25519, Ed25519) with zero wrong
results, and the hand-written assembler was audited and is ABI-correct. An
emulator (Amiberry, exact 68000/68020) ran clean too. No software defect
was found; the single early event was most likely a transient 68080 state.
The 68020 programs are shipped as the conservative choice and are verified
clean; the generic 68000 library is audited and stress-tested for real
68000 machines.

Upstream: https://franke.ms/git/bebbo/bebbossh
Base: commit 057c7f3 ("cleanup and release 1.45").

CHANGES (all as patches in src/)

0001  Non-interactive use (scripts, file transfer, exit code)
      - downloads ("bebbossh -T host cmd >file") were cut off at 64 KiB
        boundaries: the client quit on "exit-status" instead of waiting
        for CHANNEL_CLOSE
      - the remote exit code is now returned as the AmigaDOS return code
      - uploads ("bebbossh -T host cmd <file") read the whole file into a
        35 KB buffer (crash on larger files) and never sent EOF (remote
        commands such as cat/md5sum hung); now 16 KiB chunks, flow control
        and EOF
      - OpenSSH opens sessions with window 0 - the WINDOW_ADJUST is no
        longer dropped; channel 0 is confirmed and only removed on CLOSE

0002  Character set conversion in interactive sessions
      UTF-8 (server) <-> system codeset (Amiga console), via
      codesets.library if present (follows the system codeset, readable
      replacements for unmappable characters), otherwise built-in
      ISO-8859-1. Only if input AND output are a console - redirected
      data is never changed. LANG=C is only sent for non-interactive use.
      Note: codesets.library 6.22 frees a caller-supplied CSA_Dest buffer
      when CSA_MapForeignChars expands a character - worked around.

0003  Window title / OSC sequences
      All OSC sequences (ESC ] n ; ... BEL or ESC \) are removed from the
      output, OSC 0 and 2 set the window title (oh-my-zsh etc.). The old
      code only knew "ESC ]0;" and could loop forever on a title split
      across packets.

0004  Uploads from RAM:
      The argument line in the input buffer was always flushed; on a
      redirected file Flush() seeks before the start of the file, and the
      OS 3.2 RAM handler then delivers foreign memory (~70 bytes of
      garbage in front of the data). Now only done for a console.

0005  Out-of-bounds writes in libcryptossh and bebbosshkeygen
      - barrett_reduce() (Ed25519, used for every key generation and
        signature) wrote one element past a stack array
      - bebbosshkeygen overflowed its base64 buffer for comments from
        ~150 characters; output file names are now length-checked
      Found with AddressSanitizer on a Linux build.

0006  Downloads into a file started from a shell
      The OSC filter (0003) and the "report mouse/resize events" request
      were tied to stdin being a console. "bebbossh host "cat x" >file"
      typed in a shell therefore lost every byte from an ESC ] up to the
      next BEL, and 10 bytes of escape sequence ended up at the start of
      the file. Both now only apply if the output is a console. Also
      fixes a use-after-free when a channel is closed.

0007  bebboscp (SFTP copy)
      - uploads truncate the remote file; overwriting a longer file left
        its old tail behind
      - the reply to every write is checked; a full disk or quota was
        ignored and the copy reported success
      - exit code 10 if a copy step fails (it was always 0)
      - an existing remote file as target is overwritten (it tried
        "<file>/<name>" and failed)
      - "dir/" as local target wrote into the parent directory
      - remote names with '/' or ':' are skipped, a malicious server
        could otherwise write outside the target directory
      - a one-byte heap overflow parsing user@host:path, wrong speed and
        time display after a minute boundary

0008  Random numbers (libcryptossh) and bebbosshkeygen
      The old randfill() mixed rand(), the DateStamp tick and the beam
      position - a few dozen bits of entropy. It made the ed25519 secret
      keys of bebbosshkeygen and the x25519 keys of every session, so
      both could be recovered with moderate effort. Now a SHA-512 pool
      collects E-clock and beam position samples across task switches,
      exec counters and a seed file ENVARC:ssh/random_seed (renewed on
      every run). Without the seed file - first run on a fresh system -
      the keys depend on the timing jitter alone.
      bebbosshkeygen: answers from a redirected file work, write errors
      are detected (no truncated key file), the secret key is cleared
      from memory.

0009  ENVARC:ssh instead of envarc:.ssh
      Keys, known_hosts, ssh_config, authorized_keys and the random seed
      are now in ENVARC:ssh - the usual Amiga form, and the directory
      bebbosshd already uses. If only the old envarc:.ssh exists it is
      still used, so nothing breaks before you rename it (INSTALLATION).

0010  Host key check
      - a host key that differs from known_hosts is refused with a clear
        warning, like OpenSSH; it was a "yes" prompt that replaced the
        entry. If the change is expected, delete the host's line from
        ENVARC:ssh/known_hosts.
      - an unknown host is only asked about at a console; the answer was
        also read from redirected input
      - signatures with a non-canonical S (S >= L) are rejected
        (RFC 8032)

0011  Hardened packet parsing (malicious/broken server)
      String and data lengths sent by the server were used without
      bounds checks. The USERAUTH_BANNER length was written as a NUL
      terminator at an attacker-chosen offset (out-of-bounds write);
      channel data length and the key-exchange reply fields were trusted
      as well, and the length checks there wrapped at 32 bit on m68k.
      All of these are now bounds-checked against the received packet.

0012  Crypto hardening
      - the AEAD tag is compared in constant time (no timing leak)
      - an all-zero X25519 shared secret (low-order server key, which
        would make the session keys predictable) is refused
      - the session keys, the key-exchange secret and the user's private
        key are wiped from memory on exit (AmigaOS keeps freed memory)

0013  bebbosshd: hardened packet parsing (server)
      Every length a client sends is bounds-checked against the packet,
      in the handshake, the user-auth request and the channel messages;
      a malformed packet drops the session instead of reading out of
      bounds. Affects the server only.

0014  bebbosshd: public-key authentication bypass fixed (server)
      A public-key QUERY (no signature) only asks whether a key would be
      accepted. The server acknowledged it with PK_OK but then treated
      the session as logged in - so naming any key from authorized_keys
      (public keys are not secret) was enough to get in without the
      private key. The login is now granted only after the signature in
      the following request is verified.

0015  bebbosshd: SFTP/shell bounds and AllowTcpForwarding (server)
      Post-authentication hardening from a full server audit: SFTP read
      length clamped to the reply buffer; SFTP write length checked
      against the packet; file-attribute parsing bounded; the shell
      input-forward buffer capped; new config AllowTcpForwarding to
      refuse port forwarding. See RUNNING THE SERVER.

0016  bebbosshd: authentication required before the connection protocol
      Found by coverage-guided fuzzing. CHANNEL_OPEN/REQUEST were handled
      in the AUTH state too, so a client that had only done the key
      exchange could open a session and run a command WITHOUT logging in
      (unauthenticated command execution; on Linux a getpwnam(NULL)
      crash). Channels are now refused until authentication succeeds.

0017  Merge upstream 1.46: normalize the X25519 shared secret
      Upstream 1.46 canonicalizes the shared secret K as an RFC 4251
      mpint. The old code only added the sign byte and never stripped
      leading zero bytes, so a K starting with 0x00 (about 1 in 256
      connections) was hashed non-canonically - the exchange hash then
      differed from OpenSSH and the handshake failed with "incorrect
      signature". (Our call sites also set size=32 first, which upstream's
      own 1.46 omits.) This brings the build to version 1.46.

0018  Linux build only
      amiemul.h defines APTR, LONG and RETURN_* so the native Linux
      programs build again. No change to the Amiga programs.

0019  Version 1.46.1
      The programs report 1.46.1 (Version, -?, the SSH identification
      SSH-2.0-BebboSSH-1.46.1), so this build can be told apart from the
      original 1.46. libcryptossh.library keeps version 1.46: its
      version number must be numeric.

SECURITY NOTE

bebbosshd is now included. The server code was reviewed: the packet parsing
was hardened (0013), a public-key authentication bypass was fixed (0014),
the SFTP/shell handlers were bounds-checked (0015), and an unauthenticated
command-execution path was found by coverage-guided fuzzing and fixed
(0016). It was fuzzed under AddressSanitizer (parsers, SFTP, shell, crypto;
coverage-guided for the auth path) but that is not a proof of correctness,
and AmigaOS has no per-user file permissions, so any account that can log in
has full access to every mounted volume. Run it on a trusted local network
only, never exposed to the internet. An authenticated user can also use port
forwarding (disable it with AllowTcpForwarding no). Tested on a home LAN:
public-key login, exec, and SFTP up/download.

Keys made with the original bebbosshkeygen (comment "Amiga" by default)
are weak, see 0008: create a new key and replace the old one in the
server's authorized_keys.

INSTALLATION

  Copy C:bebbossh C:bebbossh.orig                       ; keep originals
  Copy C:bebbosshkeygen C:bebbosshkeygen.orig
  Copy LIBS:libcryptossh.library LIBS:libcryptossh.library.orig
  Copy C:bebboscp C:bebboscp.orig                       ; if installed
  Copy bebbossh bebboscp bebbosshkeygen C:
  ; the server is optional, see RUNNING THE SERVER:
  ; Copy bebbosshd C:
  Copy libcryptossh.library020 LIBS:libcryptossh.library  ; 68020-68040, 68080
  ; or: Copy libcryptossh.library060 LIBS:libcryptossh.library   (68060)
  Avail FLUSH                                           ; drop the old one
  Rename ENVARC:.ssh ENVARC:ssh                         ; once, see 0009

SERVER SIDE (Linux/OpenSSH)

Many sshd configurations ignore LANG from the client. With charset
conversion the session should run with a UTF-8 locale, e.g. in ~/.zshrc
or ~/.bashrc:

  [[ "$TERM" == "xterm-amiga" ]] && export LANG=de_DE.UTF-8

RUNNING THE SERVER (bebbosshd)

Read the SECURITY note first - trusted LAN only. Setup in ENVARC:ssh:

  Copy bebbosshd C:
  MakeDir ENVARC:ssh                                    ; if missing
  bebbosshkeygen -f ENVARC:ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key -C host
  ; put the client public keys into ENVARC:ssh/authorized_keys
  ; (one "ssh-ed25519 <base64> comment" line each)

ENVARC:ssh/sshd_config (no ListenAddress - the Roadshow stack binds all
interfaces; a specific address or 127.0.0.1 fails to bind):

  Port 2222
  Ciphers chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com,aes128-gcm@openssh.com
  HostKey ENVARC:ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key
  AllowTcpForwarding no        ; refuse port forwarding (default: yes)
  DebugLevel info

Start it (stop with Break <cli>):

  Run >NIL: C:bebbosshd

Password login uses ENVARC:ssh/passwd; public-key login uses
ENVARC:ssh/authorized_keys.

Access notes: a logged-in user reaches every mounted volume (AmigaOS has
no per-user file rights), and - unless AllowTcpForwarding no is set - can
have the server open TCP connections to other hosts. Keep this to a
trusted LAN.

BUILD

Built with bebbo's m68k-amigaos-gcc 6.5.0b (snapshot 2026-03-03, NDK
with newer headers) - the 1.45 sources do not build with older NDKs:

  git checkout 057c7f3 && git am --keep-cr src/00*.patch
  (--keep-cr: some sources use CRLF line endings)
  make CPU=-m68020 STRIP="-s -Wl,--gc-sections" Release=1 pre
  make CPU=-m68020 STRIP="-s -Wl,--gc-sections" Release=1 \
       "C++FLAGS=-fno-exceptions -fno-rtti -I<libcodesets>/include" \
       Release/bebbossh Release/bebboscp Release/bebbosshkeygen \
       Release/bebbosshd Release/libcryptossh.library
  (this is the 68020 build; the library variants like the original
   mkrelease: CPU=-m68060 for library060, no CPU option for the 68000
   library - "make clean" between the builds)

TESTED

Vampire V4 Standalone (68080), AmigaOS 3.2, codesets.library 6.22, against
OpenSSH 10.0 (Fedora 43): 1 MiB down-/uploads bit-exact (RAM:, Work:,
network volume), exit codes, interactive sessions with umlauts, window
titles, downloads into a file from an interactive shell. bebboscp: files and
directory trees in both directions, overwriting, "dir/" targets, error cases
(missing source, no permission, disk full via /dev/full) with exit code 10.
bebbosshkeygen: keys checked with OpenSSH (ssh-keygen -y, signing),
overwrite answer from a file; seed file renewed per run; logins with all
three library variants. Paths: old envarc:.ssh used while ENVARC:ssh is
missing, after renaming everything in ENVARC:ssh. Host keys: changed key
refused, unknown host without console refused, unknown host at a terminal
stored after "yes"; Ed25519 verification against OpenSSL (241 cases incl.
tampered and non-canonical signatures). Parsing hardening: login, 1 MiB
up/download and exit codes unchanged against OpenSSH, shared code clean
under AddressSanitizer. All three library variants: login and 1 MiB
transfers on the 68080. Amiberry (68000, 68020): 12 sessions each with up-
and downloads, bit-exact. Not tested on a real 68000, 68020-68040 or 68060,
nor against other servers. Crypto: ed25519 verification against OpenSSL (241
cases), constant-time tag compare, zero-DH refused, key material wiped on
exit. bebbosshd was run on the V4SA on a home LAN: public-key login, exec
and a 1 MiB download bit-exact over several sessions; its parsing was also
checked on Linux under AddressSanitizer. Not audited against a hostile
client; the public-key auth bypass is fixed (0014, see SECURITY).

LICENSE

GPLv3+ like the original (see COPYING). The complete source changes are
in src/; the unchanged sources are available from the upstream
repository above.
